    "title": {
      "en": "Mode, median and cumulative frequency"
    },
    "blocks": [
      {
        "id": "story",
        "type": "story",
        "provenance": "original",
        "title": {
          "en": "Most common and middle value"
        },
        "text": {
          "en": "Which marks occurred most often in a test? Which mark splits the class into two equal halves? Mode and median answer these for grouped data."
        }
      },
      {
        "id": "explain",
        "type": "explanation",
        "provenance": "original",
        "keepEnglishTerms": [
          "3 median = mode + 2 mean",
          "continuous classes",
          "cumulative frequency",
          "median class",
          "median formula",
          "modal class",
          "mode formula",
          "ogive"
        ],
        "text": {
          "en": "The modal class is the class with the highest frequency.\nMode = l + ((f1 - f0)/(2 f1 - f0 - f2)) x h, where l is the lower limit of the modal class, f1 its frequency, f0 and f2 the frequencies before and after, and h the width.\nThe cumulative frequency of a class is the sum of the frequencies of that class and all classes before it.\nThe median class is the class whose cumulative frequency is just greater than n/2.\nMedian = l + ((n/2 - cf)/f) x h, where cf is the cumulative frequency of the class before the median class.\nClasses must be continuous before using these formulas. Make them continuous if there are gaps.\nRelationship: 3 median = mode + 2 mean, an empirical relation.\nAn ogive is a graph of cumulative frequency, and the point where n/2 meets the curve gives the median."
        }
      },
